| **Previous** (the “if‑p‑>=‑0.45 …” rule) | **‑200** | The car is driven hard to the left early on ( `a = 0` ) and never gains enough momentum to climb the right hill. It stays stuck in the left‑hand valley and the episode always times‑out. |
| **Best so far** (the three‑line rule) | **‑135.3** (the only one that actually reaches the goal) | The car accelerates right **as soon as it has any positive velocity** and keeps accelerating right until it crosses the goal (`p ≥ 0.5`). When the car is still on the left side and moving left, the rule applies a left thrust (`a = 0`) to “pump” the car out of the deep part of the valley. This “pump‑and‑release” behaviour is exactly the classic solution for the Mountain‑Car problem. |

**Key take‑aways**

* The *only* rule that ever reaches the goal is the one that **keeps accelerating right whenever the velocity is positive**, regardless of the exact position.  
* When the car is still moving left ( `v < 0` ) we must **push left** *only* while the car is deep in the left valley (`p < –0.55`). Once we are closer to the centre (`p > –0.55`) a left thrust only slows us down and wastes the limited steps.  
* The “do‑nothing” action (`a = 1`) is never useful – the optimal policy is either full thrust left or full thrust right.  
* The threshold on velocity (`v > 0`) is enough; a small positive velocity is already enough to start the uphill climb because the engine can keep adding kinetic energy each step.

---

**2️⃣ The most effective strategy (the one we will keep and refine)**  

```
# 0 = accelerate left, 1 = no thrust, 2 = accelerate right
IF position >= 0.5                     THEN action = 1        # we are on the goal hill → stop accelerating
ELIF velocity > 0                       THEN action = 2        # any right‑ward motion → keep pushing right
ELIF position < -0.55 AND velocity < 0  THEN action = 0        # deep in the left valley, still moving left → pump left
ELSE                                    THEN action = 2        # otherwise (close to centre but still left‑ward) push right
```

*Why this works*  

* **Goal check** – As soon as we cross the goal (`p ≥ 0.5`) we stop accelerating; the episode ends immediately, giving the highest possible reward.  
* **Positive velocity** – As soon as the car has any right‑ward speed we keep giving maximum right thrust. This maximises the kinetic energy that will later be converted into potential energy on the hill.  
* **Deep‑left pump** – When the car is far left (`p < –0.55`) and still moving left, we keep accelerating left. This “pumping” builds up enough speed to later overcome the hill.  
* **Otherwise** – If we are not deep left but still have a left‑ward velocity, a left thrust would only waste steps; it is better to start pushing right again so that the next positive‑velocity condition will be satisfied.

---

**3️⃣ Minor tweaks that can improve the return further**

