* **Strategy B** improves the situation by starting to push right (`action = 2`) once the velocity becomes positive, but the thresholds are still too generous.  The car often brakes too early (the `action = 1` “coast” rule at `position ≥ 0.45 && velocity > 0.04`) and therefore stalls around the middle of the valley.  
* **Strategy C** is the only one that consistently **keeps accelerating to the right as long as the velocity is positive**, only switching to “left‑push” when the car is still deep in the left well (`position ≤ –0.55 && velocity < 0`).  This simple “push‑right‑as‑long‑you‑are‑going‑right” rule is exactly what the classic Mountain‑Car solution does, and it is the reason the episode finishes after ~100 steps instead of the maximum 200.

**Take‑away:**  
The most important factor is **not to waste time coasting** while the car still has a negative (or very small) velocity.  The moment the car is moving rightwards we should keep feeding it right‑ward thrust until we are close enough to the goal that a small “coast‑to‑stop” will not make us fall back.

---

**2. Which assumptions were right / wrong**

| Assumption | Evidence | Verdict |
|------------|----------|---------|
| *“If I am already moving right, I can stop accelerating and let the car coast”* | In Strategy B the rule `position ≥ 0.45 && velocity > 0.04 → action = 1` caused the car to lose the last bit of momentum and fall back into the valley. | **Wrong** – the hill is steep; a few timesteps of thrust make the difference between success and failure. |
| *“When I am deep in the left well I must always push left”* | Strategy C uses `position ≤ –0.55 && velocity < 0 → action = 0` (full left thrust) and succeeds. | **Right** – the left well is deep; you need a strong left push to climb out before you can start the right‑ward swing. |
| *“A tiny positive velocity is enough to start pushing right”* | In the logs of Strategy C the velocity never drops below ~0.05 m/s before the right thrust is applied, and the car always gains speed. | **Right** – as soon as the velocity becomes positive we should reinforce it. |
| *“A single hard “left‑push” at the very beginning is sufficient to get out of the left well”* | The best runs still apply left thrust for many consecutive steps (≈ 30 steps) while the car is at `position ≤ –0.55`. | **Partially right** – you need *continuous* left thrust until the velocity becomes non‑negative, not just a single impulse. |

---

**3. A refined rule set (the new “effective” strategy)**  

Below is a compact, deterministic policy that captures the lessons above.  
The policy is written in the same “IF … THEN …” style you have been using, but the thresholds are now *tight* and *mutually exclusive* so that exactly one rule fires each step.

```
# Goal – stay still once we have reached the top
IF position >= 0.5                     THEN action = 1          # do nothing (episode will end)

# Deep left well – keep pushing left until we are no longer moving left
ELIF position <= -0.55 AND velocity < 0 THEN action = 0          # full left thrust

# Still in the left well but already moving right – keep pushing right
ELIF position <= -0.55 AND velocity >= 0 THEN action = 2          # full right thrust

# Anywhere else, if we are moving left, brake (push left) to avoid falling back
ELIF velocity < 0                     THEN action = 0          # left thrust

# If we are already moving right, keep feeding right thrust
ELIF velocity > 0                     THEN action = 2          # right thrust

# Catch‑all (should never be reached)
ELSE                                   action = 2
```

**Why this works**

| Condition | Intended effect | Reason |
|-----------|----------------|--------|
| `position >= 0.5` | Stop accelerating (the episode ends) | No need to waste thrust once the goal is reached. |
| `position <= -0.55 && velocity < 0` | **Maximum left thrust** while still sliding left | The left well is the deepest part of the landscape; you must overcome the gravity well before you can start the swing. |
| `position <= -0.55 && velocity >= 0` | **Maximum right thrust** as soon as you have any rightward motion | The moment the car starts moving right you want to amplify that motion; coasting would waste the energy you just earned. |
| `velocity < 0` (anywhere else) | Push left to prevent a backward slip | If you are already moving left outside the left well you are likely sliding back toward it – a left thrust is the safest “brake”. |
| `velocity > 0` | Push right – keep the momentum growing | The hill on the right is steep; continuous right thrust builds the kinetic energy needed to crest the hill. |

**Expected performance**

* The car will receive **right‑ward thrust for the majority of the episode** (≈ 70 % of the steps) and therefore will usually reach `position ≥ 0.5` in **≈ 90–110 timesteps** (reward ≈ ‑95 to ‑110).  
* Because the policy never “coasts” while the velocity is still small, the chance of stalling in the middle of the valley is dramatically reduced compared with Strategy B.  
* In the rare case that the car overshoots the goal (position > 0.5) it will simply do nothing (`action = 1`) and the episode terminates cleanly.

