| **Very small positive velocity** (0 < v < 0.02) while still left of the right hill (x < 0.3) | `action = 2` (full thrust) | **Add a tiny “coast‑if‑almost‑there” clause**: <br>`ELIF position >= 0.25 AND velocity > 0 AND velocity < 0.03 → action = 1` | When the car has already built enough speed to clear the hill, a small coast avoids overshooting the 0.5 threshold and therefore saves the final step. |
| **Right‑hand valley bottom** (‑0.2 ≤ x < 0) with a tiny negative velocity (‑0.01 > v > ‑0.03) | `action = 0` (push left) | **Add a “push‑right‑instead” clause**: <br>`ELIF -0.2 <= position < 0 AND -0.03 <= velocity < 0 → action = 2` | In this region the slope already points right; a small left thrust can actually waste the momentum we have just earned. |
| **Stalling near the leftmost edge** (x ≈ ‑0.55, |v| < 0.01) | `action = 0` (push left) | **Add a “hard‑push‑left” clause**: <br>`ELIF position <= -0.55 AND abs(velocity) < 0.01 → action = 0` (same as before but keep it explicit) | Guarantees that the car never gets stuck with a near‑zero velocity in the deepest part of the valley. |

These refinements keep the rule set **still human‑readable** (≤ 10 lines) while targeting the two most common failure modes observed in the traces: (i) a tiny overshoot after the hill is reached, and (ii) unnecessary left thrust when the slope already favors the right direction.

